  <h2 id="pay-perks">Pay and perks compared across all four roles</h2>

  <p>
    Salary comparisons for RBI and SBI tend to quote whichever number makes one side look best. Here is the
    realistic entry-level pay and perk package for each of the four roles.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Role</th>
          <th>Reported starting pay</th>
          <th>Core perks</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {payRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Role"><strong>{item.role}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Reported starting pay">{item.pay}</td>
            <td data-label="Core perks">{item.perks}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      RBI Grade B's gross salary advantage over SBI PO is commonly reported at close to 60% at entry level.
      That gap is real, but it sits alongside a vacancy pool that is a fraction of SBI PO's — often 60-350
      Grade B posts a cycle against roughly 1,500 SBI PO posts in the 2026 notification. Weigh the pay gap
      against your realistic odds of clearing each specific exam, not just the headline number.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="promotion-ladders">Promotion ladders side by side</h2>

  <p>
    Both organisations run genuine, structured promotion systems rather than vague "grow with us" language —
    but the shape and pace of each ladder differs.
  </p>

  <p>
    RBI's officer ladder runs from Grade B through Grade C, Grade D, and Grade E toward senior positions
    like Executive Director, with Deputy Governor and Governor sitting at the very top, reachable in
    practice by only a small number of career RBI officers or external appointees over a career. Promotion
    depends on departmental exams, performance appraisals, and fast-track schemes for strong performers.
    RBI Assistants can move into the officer cadre through a Normal-Based route (tied to service years and
    internal exam scores) or a Merit-Based route (available after 3 years of service if you hold JAIIB and
    CAIIB alongside a graduate or postgraduate degree), and from there climb the same Grade B-and-above
    ladder as direct recruits.
  </p>

  <p>
    SBI's officer ladder runs from Probationary Officer through Manager, Senior Manager, Chief Manager,
    Assistant General Manager, Deputy General Manager, and Chief General Manager, toward Deputy Managing
    Director and Managing Director for the strongest performers, typically reaching AGM or DGM level within
    10-15 years for meritorious officers. SBI Clerks can be promoted into Junior Management Grade Scale-I
    officer roles after 6 years under a fast-track channel or 12 years under the normal channel, once JAIIB
    and CAIIB qualifications, an internal written exam, and an interview are cleared.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Neither ladder rewards seniority alone. In both organisations, the officers who reach the top fastest
      are the ones who keep clearing internal departmental or promotion exams year after year, not the ones
      who assume time served will carry them there automatically.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Use The 4-Checkpoint Protocol before you commit prep time to either track</h2>

  <p>
    A salary table or a promotion chart cannot tell you which organisation fits your actual working style
    and life situation. The 4-Checkpoint Protocol narrows this decision to what genuinely matters for you.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {checkpointItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
