          <p>{item.body}</p>
          <div class="checkpoint__note">{item.note}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    If you are still unsure after running this test honestly, use the
    <a href="/career-skills-compass/">Career & Skills Compass</a> to check whether your natural working
    style leans toward analytical, policy-facing work or people-facing, target-driven work before you lock
    in months of exam prep.
  </p>

  <h2 id="prestige-ceiling">Prestige and career ceiling, honestly</h2>

  <p>
    Within Indian banking and finance circles, RBI Grade B is widely regarded as the more prestigious
    credential, largely because it sits inside the institution that regulates every other bank in the
    country, including SBI. That prestige is genuine — it shows up in how recruiters, business schools, and
    finance professionals treat an RBI Grade B background compared to a commercial bank officer background.
  </p>

  <p>
    But prestige is not the same as career ceiling for everyone. SBI, as India's largest commercial bank,
    offers a genuinely large number of senior leadership posts across a vast branch and business network —
    more total officer-level positions exist inside SBI at any given time than inside RBI's much smaller
    officer cadre. A strong SBI officer can realistically reach AGM or DGM level and manage a business
    portfolio worth thousands of crores. A strong RBI officer can realistically reach Executive Director and
    shape supervisory or monetary policy that affects the entire banking sector.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      If prestige inside the finance world matters more to you than anything else, RBI Grade B is the
      stronger credential. If you want the widest set of senior leadership opportunities across the largest
      possible organisation, SBI's scale gives you more total officer posts to compete for over a career.
      Neither answer is universally "better" — they measure different things.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="daily-work">What the job actually looks like day to day</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part salary charts and prestige rankings both skip, and it matters more than either once
    you are a few years in.
  </p>

  <p>
    A typical RBI Grade B posting involves reviewing bank compliance filings, analysing currency and forex
    data, supporting monetary policy research, or supervising regional office operations, on a fixed
    five-day schedule with limited public contact. The pace is steady and analytical rather than
    transaction-driven.
  </p>

  <p>
    A typical SBI PO posting involves appraising loan applications, meeting deposit and cross-selling
    targets, managing a team of clerical staff, and handling customer escalations, often on branch hours
    that extend around month-end and target periods. The pace is faster, more people-facing, and more
    immediately measured by visible business numbers.
  </p>

  <p>
    RBI Assistants spend their days on internal data verification and record-keeping with no customer
    contact at all, while SBI Clerks spend their days at a teller counter handling deposits, withdrawals,
    and account queries directly with the public — arguably the sharpest day-to-day contrast in this entire
    comparison.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Build a high-income skill portfolio alongside whichever ladder you pick.</strong> A stable RBI
      or SBI pay cheque removes income anxiety, which is genuinely valuable — but neither role, by itself,
      is the fastest route to earlier financial freedom. Officers who also build a second, market-facing
      skill (financial modelling, data analysis, communication, or a specialised certification relevant to
      their department) tend to unlock better internal postings, panel interviews, and — if they ever choose
      to leave — stronger outside offers than officers who rely on seniority alone.
    </p>
  </div>

